<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine Tikh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Constantine Tikh Asen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine Tikh was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1257 to 1277, chosen by the boyars after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-asen">Michael II Asen</a>. A noble of mixed Bulgarian and Serbian descent, he married a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> and later a Byzantine princess to strengthen his claim. His reign saw Mongol raids and Byzantine pressure, and he was killed in 1277 by the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivailo">Ivailo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-tikh.jpg" alt="Constantine Tikh" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bichoes78 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KonstantinTih.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Konstantin Tih was a wealthy Bulgarian boyar whose estates lay in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>. In a charter to the Saint George Monastery near Skopje he stated that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was his grandfather, and the Byzantine historian George Pachymeres called him half-Serbian. He may have been related to the Serbian royal house through either parent. Some scholars hold that he was a son or nephew of the boyar John Tihomir, who controlled Skopje in the late 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-asen">Michael II Asen</a> was murdered by his cousin Kaliman in late 1256 or early 1257, and Kaliman was soon killed too, ending the male line of the Asen dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostislav-mikhailovich">Rostislav Mikhailovich</a> of Macsó and the boyar Mitso claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but neither won the support of the boyars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, who offered the throne to Konstantin. He divorced his first wife and in 1258 married Irene Doukaina Laskarina, daughter of Theodore II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and Elena, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a>. The marriage strengthened his claim, and he was thereafter called Konstantin Asen.</p><p>War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> followed. In 1260 he retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> from Rostislav, and in 1261 Hungarian troops under Stephen, son of Béla IV, stormed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and restored the north-west to Rostislav. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> blinded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris">John IV Laskaris</a>, Irene persuaded Konstantin to fight Michael.</p><p>He took Stanimaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> in 1262, but a Byzantine army recovered them, and by the end of 1263 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> had lost significant territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the Hungarians. In late 1264 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> forces against Michael and took the fortress of Ainos. In 1266 Stephen captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> and plundered the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><p>In 1268, to keep him out of an anti-Byzantine coalition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> gave him his niece Maria Palaiologina Kantakouzene as a third wife and promised Mesembria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomorie">Anchialos</a> as her dowry, but did not hand them over. A riding accident left Konstantin paralysed from the waist down, and Maria took control of the government. Nogai's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> regularly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Costly and unsuccessful wars, repeated Mongol raids and economic instability, including Konstantin's minting of coins on a vast scale, led to a revolt in 1277. A swineherd or swine-owner named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivailo">Ivaylo</a> led the discontented and took control of a significant area. Konstantin marched against him with his guard but was decisively defeated and killed in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>. His son Michael Tih by Maria succeeded as co-emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1277 to 1279.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine I Tih” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mongol attacks from 1242 forced the tsars to pay tribute, and in 1277 the swineherd Ivailo led a peasant rising that beat off Mongol raiders and killed Tsar <b>Constantine Tikh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Died in the Second Bulgarian Empire. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42,21.43&amp;year=1267" aria-label="Skopje on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5071 669 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine Tikh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 939h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 939h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42,21.43&amp;year=1267">What was here in 1267? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
